        "## 1. Objectives"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This activity aims to introduce the convolutional process by demonstrating with the programmatic processing of both arbitrarily generated signals and collected data using simple convolution techniques."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "do-8nSpXFpyd"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 2. Intended Learning Outcomes (ILOs)\n",
        "After completion of this activity, the students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Develop a program that manipulates signals with convolutional processing with simple response signals."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "viGgXkpCpjvc"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 3. Discussion\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "DpTydQHT7LEK"
      },
      "source": [
        "In mathematics (in particular, functional analysis), convolution is a mathematical operation on two functions (f and g) that produces a third function (f*g) that expresses how the shape of one is modified by the other. The term convolution refers to both the result function and to the process of computing it. It is defined as the integral of the product of the two functions after one is reversed and shifted. The integral is evaluated for all values of shift, producing the convolution function.\n",
        "\n",
        "Convolution has applications that include probability, statistics, acoustics, spectroscopy, signal processing and image processing, engineering, physics, computer vision and differential equations.\n",
        "\n",
        "The convolution can be defined for functions on Euclidean space and other groups.For example, periodic functions, such as the discrete-time Fourier transform, can be defined on a circle and convolved by periodic convolution. A discrete convolution can be defined for functions on the set of integers.\n",
        "\n",
        "Generalizations of convolution have applications in the field of numerical analysis and numerical linear algebra, and in the design and implementation of finite impulse response filters in signal processing. Computing the inverse of the convolution operation is known as deconvolution."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "X-RNZovNGV9k"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 4. Resources\n",
        "The activity will require the following software, tools and equipment:\n",
        "* IDE or any online code editor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "o5NQIlrAqem8"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 5. Directions:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 1. Programatically generate the following signals from index [-20,20]:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* A delta function"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Define the index range [-20, 20]\nn = np.arange(-20, 21)\n\n# Delta function\ndelta = np.zeros_like(n, dtype=float)\ndelta[n == 0] = 1.0\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, delta)\nplt.title('Delta Function')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* An exponential signal with negative \"a\""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Exponential signal with negative \"a\" (a = -0.1)\na = -0.1\nexponential = np.exp(a * n)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, exponential)\nplt.title('Exponential Signal (a = -0.1)')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* A ramp signal"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Ramp signal\nramp = np.maximum(0, n)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, ramp)\nplt.title('Ramp Signal')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* A sinusoidal wave with 0.05 Hz and unit amplitude"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Sinusoidal wave with 0.05 Hz and unit amplitude\nfreq = 0.05\namplitude = 1.0\nsinusoidal = amplitude * np.sin(2 * np.pi * freq * n)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, sinusoidal)\nplt.title('Sinusoidal Wave (0.05 Hz, Unit Amplitude)')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 2. Add noise to each signal using `np.random` between 0-1. Then plot the four generated signals."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* Delta function"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "np.random.seed(42)  # For reproducibility\n\n# Add noise to delta function\nnoise_delta = np.random.uniform(0, 1, size=len(n))\nnoisy_delta = delta + noise_delta\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, noisy_delta)\nplt.title('Delta Function + Noise')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* Exponential signal with negative \"a\""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Add noise to exponential signal\nnoise_exponential = np.random.uniform(0, 1, size=len(n))\nnoisy_exponential = exponential + noise_exponential\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, noisy_exponential)\nplt.title('Exponential Signal + Noise')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* Ramp signal"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Add noise to ramp signal\nnoise_ramp = np.random.uniform(0, 1, size=len(n))\nnoisy_ramp = ramp + noise_ramp\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, noisy_ramp)\nplt.title('Ramp Signal + Noise')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "* Sinusoidal wave with 0.05 Hz and unit amplitude"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Add noise to sinusoidal signal\nnoise_sinusoidal = np.random.uniform(0, 1, size=len(n))\nnoisy_sinusoidal = sinusoidal + noise_sinusoidal\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.stem(n, noisy_sinusoidal)\nplt.title('Sinusoidal Wave + Noise')\nplt.xlabel('n')\nplt.ylabel('Amplitude')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 3. Perform a convolution using summation formula (not using `np.convolve` or any libraries) for four generated signals using the following impulse response with values A = [0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0]. Plot and save the images."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def convolve(x, h):\n    \"\"\"\n    Perform convolution using summation formula (not using np.convolve).\n    \n    Formula: y[n] = sum_{k} x[k] * h[n-k]\n    \"\"\"\n    nx = len(x)\n    nh = len(h)\n    ny = nx + nh - 1\n    y = np.zeros(ny)\n    \n    # Pad x with zeros\n    x_padded = np.pad(x, (nh-1, nh-1), mode='constant')\n    \n    # Flip h for convolution\n    h_flipped = h[::-1]\n    \n    # Perform convolution using sliding window\n    for i in range(ny):\n        y[i] = np.sum(x_padded[i:i+nh] * h_flipped)\n    \n    return y\n\n# Define impulse response A\nA = np.array([0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0])\n\n# Create directory for outputs\nimport os\nos.makedirs('outputs/kernel_A', exist_ok=True)\n\n# Convolve each noisy signal with kernel A\nsignals = {'delta': noisy_delta, 'exponential': noisy_exponential, \n           'ramp': noisy_ramp, 'sinusoidal': noisy_sinusoidal}\n\nfor name, signal in signals.items():\n    convolved = convolve(signal, A)\n    n_conv = np.arange(len(convolved))\n    \n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(12, 8))\n    fig.suptitle(f'Convolution with Kernel A: {name.capitalize()} Signal', fontsize=14)\n    \n    axes[0].stem(n, signal)\n    axes[0].set_title(f'Original Noisy {name.capitalize()} Signal')\n    axes[0].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[0].grid(True)\n    \n    axes[1].stem(n_conv, convolved)\n    axes[1].set_title(f'Convolved Signal with Kernel A')\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[1].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[1].grid(True)\n    \n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(f'outputs/kernel_A/convolution_{name}_kernelA.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n    plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### B = [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Define impulse response B (Moving Average)\nB = np.array([1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\nos.makedirs('outputs/kernel_B', exist_ok=True)\n\n# Convolve each noisy signal with kernel B\nfor name, signal in signals.items():\n    convolved = convolve(signal, B)\n    n_conv = np.arange(len(convolved))\n    \n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(12, 8))\n    fig.suptitle(f'Convolution with Kernel B: {name.capitalize()} Signal', fontsize=14)\n    \n    axes[0].stem(n, signal)\n    axes[0].set_title(f'Original Noisy {name.capitalize()} Signal')\n    axes[0].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[0].grid(True)\n    \n    axes[1].stem(n_conv, convolved)\n    axes[1].set_title(f'Convolved Signal with Kernel B (Moving Average)')\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[1].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[1].grid(True)\n    \n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(f'outputs/kernel_B/convolution_{name}_kernelB.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n    plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### C = [-1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1]\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Define impulse response C (Inverted Moving Average)\nC = np.array([-1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1])\nos.makedirs('outputs/kernel_C', exist_ok=True)\n\n# Convolve each noisy signal with kernel C\nfor name, signal in signals.items():\n    convolved = convolve(signal, C)\n    n_conv = np.arange(len(convolved))\n    \n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(12, 8))\n    fig.suptitle(f'Convolution with Kernel C: {name.capitalize()} Signal', fontsize=14)\n    \n    axes[0].stem(n, signal)\n    axes[0].set_title(f'Original Noisy {name.capitalize()} Signal')\n    axes[0].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[0].grid(True)\n    \n    axes[1].stem(n_conv, convolved)\n    axes[1].set_title(f'Convolved Signal with Kernel C (Inverted Moving Average)')\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[1].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[1].grid(True)\n    \n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(f'outputs/kernel_C/convolution_{name}_kernelC.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n    plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### D = [0, 1, 0, -1, 0, 1, 0]\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Define impulse response D (Differentiator-like)\nD = np.array([0, 1, 0, -1, 0, 1, 0])\nos.makedirs('outputs/kernel_D', exist_ok=True)\n\n# Convolve each noisy signal with kernel D\nfor name, signal in signals.items():\n    convolved = convolve(signal, D)\n    n_conv = np.arange(len(convolved))\n    \n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(12, 8))\n    fig.suptitle(f'Convolution with Kernel D: {name.capitalize()} Signal', fontsize=14)\n    \n    axes[0].stem(n, signal)\n    axes[0].set_title(f'Original Noisy {name.capitalize()} Signal')\n    axes[0].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[0].grid(True)\n    \n    axes[1].stem(n_conv, convolved)\n    axes[1].set_title(f'Convolved Signal with Kernel D (Differentiator-like)')\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[1].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[1].grid(True)\n    \n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(f'outputs/kernel_D/convolution_{name}_kernelD.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n    plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### E = [1, -1, 1, -1, 1, -1, 1]\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Define impulse response E (High-pass filter)\nE = np.array([1, -1, 1, -1, 1, -1, 1])\nos.makedirs('outputs/kernel_E', exist_ok=True)\n\n# Convolve each noisy signal with kernel E\nfor name, signal in signals.items():\n    convolved = convolve(signal, E)\n    n_conv = np.arange(len(convolved))\n    \n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(12, 8))\n    fig.suptitle(f'Convolution with Kernel E: {name.capitalize()} Signal', fontsize=14)\n    \n    axes[0].stem(n, signal)\n    axes[0].set_title(f'Original Noisy {name.capitalize()} Signal')\n    axes[0].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[0].grid(True)\n    \n    axes[1].stem(n_conv, convolved)\n    axes[1].set_title(f'Convolved Signal with Kernel E (High-pass filter)')\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[1].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[1].grid(True)\n    \n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(f'outputs/kernel_E/convolution_{name}_kernelE.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n    plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### F = [0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.5, 0.4, 0.2, 0]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Define impulse response F (Gaussian-like smoothing)\nF = np.array([0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.5, 0.4, 0.2, 0])\nos.makedirs('outputs/kernel_F', exist_ok=True)\n\n# Convolve each noisy signal with kernel F\nfor name, signal in signals.items():\n    convolved = convolve(signal, F)\n    n_conv = np.arange(len(convolved))\n    \n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(12, 8))\n    fig.suptitle(f'Convolution with Kernel F: {name.capitalize()} Signal', fontsize=14)\n    \n    axes[0].stem(n, signal)\n    axes[0].set_title(f'Original Noisy {name.capitalize()} Signal')\n    axes[0].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[0].grid(True)\n    \n    axes[1].stem(n_conv, convolved)\n    axes[1].set_title(f'Convolved Signal with Kernel F (Gaussian-like smoothing)')\n    axes[1].set_xlabel('n')\n    axes[1].set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    axes[1].grid(True)\n    \n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(f'outputs/kernel_F/convolution_{name}_kernelF.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n    plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 5. Compare and analyze the different effects of each impulse response to the original signal and each other."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Comparison plot showing all kernels applied to the sinusoidal signal\nkernels = {'A': A, 'B': B, 'C': C, 'D': D, 'E': E, 'F': F}\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(3, 2, figsize=(14, 12))\nfig.suptitle('Comparison of All Kernels Applied to Sinusoidal Signal', fontsize=14)\n\nfor ax, (kernel_name, kernel) in zip(axes.flatten(), kernels.items()):\n    convolved = convolve(noisy_sinusoidal, kernel)\n    n_conv = np.arange(len(convolved))\n    ax.stem(n_conv, convolved)\n    ax.set_title(f'Kernel {kernel_name}: {kernel}')\n    ax.set_xlabel('n')\n    ax.set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n    ax.grid(True)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('outputs/5_comparison_all_kernels_sinusoidal.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()\n\n# Analysis:\nprint(\"Analysis of Kernel Effects:\")\nprint(\"- Kernel A (Delta): Preserves the original signal shape, acts as an identity filter\")\nprint(\"- Kernel B (Moving Average): Smooths the signal, reduces noise but blurs transitions\")\nprint(\"- Kernel C (Inverted Average): Inverts and smooths the signal, flips the polarity\")\nprint(\"- Kernel D (Differentiator): Emphasizes changes/transitions in the signal\")\nprint(\"- Kernel E (High-pass): Amplifies high-frequency components, enhances rapid changes\")\nprint(\"- Kernel F (Gaussian): Provides smooth Gaussian filtering, reduces noise effectively\")"
      ],
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 6. Provide your conclusion for each impulse response."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Conclusions for Each Impulse Response:**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Kernel A [0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0]:** This is a delta-like kernel that acts as an identity filter. When convolved with any signal, it preserves the original signal shape without modification. The output is essentially the same as the input signal, making it useful for signal verification or as a baseline comparison.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Kernel B [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]:** This is a moving average filter that smooths the signal by averaging neighboring samples. It effectively reduces high-frequency noise but also blurs sharp transitions and edges in the signal. The smoothing effect becomes more pronounced with larger kernel sizes.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Kernel C [-1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1]:** This is an inverted moving average filter. Like Kernel B, it smooths the signal but also inverts the polarity (flips the sign). The output is the negative of the smoothed signal, which can be useful for creating inverse effects or edge detection in certain applications.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Kernel D [0, 1, 0, -1, 0, 1, 0]:** This kernel acts as a differentiator-like filter that emphasizes changes and transitions in the signal. It responds strongly to edges and rapid changes while suppressing constant or slowly varying components. This makes it useful for edge detection and feature extraction.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Kernel E [1, -1, 1, -1, 1, -1, 1]:** This is a high-pass filter that amplifies high-frequency components and rapid changes in the signal. It enhances transitions and edges while suppressing low-frequency components. The alternating pattern creates a strong response to rapid oscillations.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Kernel F [0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.5, 0.4, 0.2, 0]:** This is a Gaussian-like smoothing filter that provides effective noise reduction while preserving signal structure better than a simple moving average. The bell-shaped weighting gives more importance to central samples, resulting in smoother filtering with less distortion."
      ],
    {
